<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miri</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miri is a city in northern Sarawak, Malaysia, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a>. It grew up around the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oil-well">oil well</a> in Sarawak, drilled by Shell in 1910, and became the centre of the Sarawak oil industry. Japanese forces landed there on 16 December 1941 to seize the oilfields, beginning the occupation of British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miri.jpg" alt="Miri" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mx. Granger · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Miri_City_Council_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Miri is a coastal city in north-eastern Sarawak, on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>, near the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a>. It lies on the alluvial plain of the Miri River. Beach drift has built up Peninsula Road as a barrier beach between the river and the shore, so that the river runs parallel to the coast before breaking through into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a>. The climate is tropical rainforest, with a dry southwest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> from April to September and a wet northeast monsoon from October to March. The town takes its name from a small ethnic group, the Jati Miriek, among the earliest settlers of the region; Europeans who came for oil mispronounced the name as Miri.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first foragers visited the West Mouth of Niah Cave, about 110 kilometres southwest of Miri, 50,000 years ago. A modern human skull nicknamed the Deep Skull, found by Tom Harrisson in 1958, is the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> and probably belongs to an adolescent girl.</p><p>In 1883 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a> ceded the Baram region, including Miri, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-brooke-rajah-of-sarawak">Charles Brooke, Rajah of Sarawak</a>. Miri was then a fishing village of about 20 scattered houses, a few Chinese shops and a lone Arab trader, and Claudetown, now Marudi, was the administrative centre. Miri was founded in 1910 when Royal Dutch Shell drilled the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oil-well">oil well</a>, and by 1929 it was the administrative centre of northern Sarawak.</p><p>As war approached, British forces withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> in 1941, and the Brooke government adopted a denial scheme to destroy the oil installations at Miri and Lutong, since the 48-kilometre coastline was impossible to defend. Miri was the first landing point of Japanese troops in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a>. The occupation made it a target for Allied air raids, which destroyed refinery facilities. Oil exploration moved offshore from the 1950s, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petronas">Petronas</a>, formed in 1974, began production-sharing contracts with Shell in 1976.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Miri is the second largest city in Sarawak, with a population of 356,900 in 2020, and the capital of Miri District. It received city status in 2005. It is the birthplace of the Malaysian petroleum industry, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oil-palm">oil palm</a> and tourism also matter. It is the gateway to Gunung Mulu National Park, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, and to the national parks of Niah, Lambir Hills and Loagan Bunut. The population includes Iban, Malay, Chinese, Melanau and many other groups, and the languages include Malay, Malaysian Mandarin and Iban.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Miri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Japanese forces landed at <b>Miri</b> in Sarawak on 16 December 1941, seeking its oilfields, and took Kuching on 24 December.</blockquote><a href="/state/japanese-occupation-of-british-borneo">Japanese occupation of British Borneo</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Shah <b>Miris</b> Islam spread widely, encouraged by Sufi missionaries such as Mir Sayyid Ali Hamadani, who brought Persian crafts including shawl-weaving and papier-mache.</blockquote><a href="/state/kashmir-sultanate">Kashmir Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
